    {
      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To find the positive value of \\( t \\) such that \\( |{-4+ti}| = 2\\sqrt{13} \\), we need to compute the magnitude of the complex number \\(-4 + ti\\). \n\nThe magnitude of a complex number \\( a + bi \\) is given by:\n\\[\n|a + bi| = \\sqrt{a^2 + b^2}\n\\]\n\nIn this case, \\( a = -4 \\) and \\( b = t \\). Therefore, the magnitude of \\(-4 + ti\\) is:\n\\[\n|-4 + ti| = \\sqrt{(-4)^2 + t^2} = \\sqrt{16 + t^2}\n\\]\n\nWe are given that this magnitude is equal to \\( 2\\sqrt{13} \\):\n\\[\n\\sqrt{16 + t^2} = 2\\sqrt{13}\n\\]\n\nTo solve for \\( t \\), start by squaring both sides of the equation:\n\\[\n16 + t^2 = (2\\sqrt{13})^2\n\\]\n\nSimplify the right-hand side:\n\\[\n16 + t^2 = 4 \\times 13\n\\]\n\\[\n16 + t^2 = 52\n\\]\n\nNext, isolate \\( t^2 \\) by subtracting 16 from both sides:\n\\[\nt^2 = 52 - 16\n\\]\n\\[\nt^2 = 36\n\\]\n\nNow, take the square root of both sides:\n\\[\nt = \\sqrt{36}\n\\]\n\\[\nt = 6\n\\]\n\nSince we need a positive value, our solution is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{6}\n\\]",
