      "text": "To solve for the positive value of \\( t \\) in the equation \\( |{-4+ti}| = 2\\sqrt{13} \\), we need to use the formula for the magnitude of a complex number. The magnitude of a complex number \\( a+bi \\) is given by \\( \\sqrt{a^2 + b^2} \\).\n\nHere, the complex number is \\( -4 + ti \\), so \\( a = -4 \\) and \\( b = t \\). The magnitude is:\n\\[\n|-4 + ti| = \\sqrt{(-4)^2 + t^2} = \\sqrt{16 + t^2}\n\\]\nWe are given that this magnitude is equal to \\( 2\\sqrt{13} \\), so we can set up the equation:\n\\[\n\\sqrt{16 + t^2} = 2\\sqrt{13}\n\\]\nTo eliminate the square root, we square both sides of the equation:\n\\[\n16 + t^2 = (2\\sqrt{13})^2\n\\]\n\\[\n16 + t^2 = 4 \\cdot 13\n\\]\n\\[\n16 + t^2 = 52\n\\]\nNext, we isolate \\( t^2 \\) by subtracting 16 from both sides:\n\\[\nt^2 = 52 - 16\n\\]\n\\[\nt^2 = 36\n\\]\nTo find \\( t \\), we take the positive square root of both sides (since we are asked for the positive value of \\( t \\)):\n\\[\nt = \\sqrt{36} = 6\n\\]\nTherefore, the positive value of \\( t \\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{6}\n\\]",
